00430 {\cf20 // A span can either represent a single span of memory pages with size declared by span_map_count configuration variable,}\par
00431 {\cf20 // or a set of spans in a continuous region, a super span. Any reference to the term "span" usually refers to both a single}\par
00432 {\cf20 // span or a super span. A super span can further be divided into multiple spans (or this, super spans), where the first}\par
00433 {\cf20 // (super)span is the master and subsequent (super)spans are subspans. The master span keeps track of how many subspans}\par
00434 {\cf20 // that are still alive and mapped in virtual memory, and once all subspans and master have been unmapped the entire}\par
00435 {\cf20 // superspan region is released and unmapped (on Windows for example, the entire superspan range has to be released}\par
00436 {\cf20 // in the same call to release the virtual memory range, but individual subranges can be decommitted individually}\par
00437 {\cf20 // to reduce physical memory use).}\par
